From: dkrieger@netcom.com (Dave Krieger)
Date: Wed, 2 Nov 1994 19:56:55 -0800
Subject: [#94-11-46] BOOK:  Seeking agent or publisher

Greetings, fellow 'tropes --

Romana Machado, Geoff Dale, and I have resolved to write what we're
presently calling "BEST DO IT SO!:  The Big Yellow Book of Extropian
Thought".  The purpose of the book is to provide:

  * a central reference for "the basics" (but avoiding "hardening of the
    orthodoxies");
  * wider awareness of the movement;
  * publicity for Extropy Institute.

The book will be intended for a mass-market audience, with a simple,
accessible style; many short chunks of text; and lots of graphics.  Our
present working outline goes like this:

 1) A Critique of Consensus Reality: Everything They Told You Is A Lie

        This section will present the basic flaws in the conventional
        worldview (especially as taught in modern government-run schools).
        We'll use, for example, the World's Smallest Political Quiz to
        point out the inconsistency of "left" and "right" viewpoints, and
        how the left-right paradigm ignores an entire dimension of the
        political map.  We'll cogently dismantle the widespread
        insanities of our society, from apocalyptic environmentalism to
        creeping state control of everything.

 2) Extropian Sciences and Technologies

        Evolutionary Processes (Bionomics)
        Austrian Economics
        Memetics
        Space Industrialization
        Nanotechnology
        Cryptography
        ... you can guess the rest

 3) Tools for Thought

        This section will discuss little-known but highly useful
        abstractions such as Schelling points, Fredkin's Paradox, E-Prime,
        pancritical rationalism, and so forth, that Extropians frequently
        use to inform their thinking and dialogue but most people have
        never encountered.

 4) Intellectual Roots of Extropian Thought

        The two "trunks" of the Extropian "family tree" -- political and
        technological -- both come down from the Enlightenment.  Starting with
        Locke and Bacon (roughly), down through Nietzsche, Rand, Stirner, the
        Austrian economists, Nock, Ettinger, Heinlein -- those who went before
        us who thought, felt, and acted as we do.

 5) History of Extropian Movement

        From two guys at USC to hundreds worldwide; from a photocopied flyer
        to the pages of the _Wall Street Journal_ and _Village Voice_...

 6) The Extropian Principles

        Presented in plain, down-to-earth language that anyone can understand.

 7) Practical Applications

        How to fight entropy now -- self-defense, diet, exercise, financial
        planning and management, maintenance of mind and body, cryonic
        suspension arrangements, getting and staying well-informed, and
        finding freedom in an unfree world.

 8) The Sixth Principle -- FUN!!

        Proving that Extropians don't take themselves too seriously -- our
        parties, our name changes, our alter egos, our extropian calendar,
        our grandiose titles, the Handshake, Futique Neologisms, our
        extropian parodies of the world around us, and the Godling's
        Glossary...

We want to begin each section with a box entitled "Cultivating a Healthy
Suspicion," full of thought-provoking questions like these:

        Who told you that economics is boring?  Why might they want you to
        think that?

        "Everybody knows" that America is the free-est country in the world.
        Which is the second-free-est?

        Why is everything you like either illegal, immoral, or fattening?

to which we will provide (our own) extropian answers.  We also want each
section to have a good bibliography at the end, providing pointers to (an
expansion of) the extropian reading list, as well as short reviews of the
more important extropian sourcebooks (_Engines_, TMOF, ...) in the text
itself.

We want to do the book in 8.5 x 11 format, with lots of sidebars,
graphics, and interesting layout (somewhat wilder than "The Economist"
but nowhere near as wild as "WiReD"); we anticipate the length will be in
the neighborhood of 200-300 pages.  We have set ourselves a deadline of 1
Jan 1996 (376 PNO for purists) for completion.

We are prepared to self-publish if we have to, but we believe, with the
present notoriety of extropians, that the iron is hot for striking, and
that this book has the potential to be a big success with a major
publisher. Toward that end, we are seeking any and all pointers to:

 1) A competent, experienced literary agent who will be enthusiastic
    about the project and has negotiated contracts for this type of book
    before;

 2) Potential publishers who would be sympathetic to the goals of
    extropianism and, if possible, able to potentiate the project with a
    cash advance. The best publisher would also be one with a CD-ROM
    imprint; we want to publish an expanded version of the book with
    material from additional sources, such as _Extropy_ back issues, books
    from the reading list, software tools, and other goodies. This material
    will come from sympathetic sources, but we would really value a
    publisher experienced in negotiating rights for this kind of
    compilation. The CD-ROM is, however, a secondary project.

Any help that list members can provide or refer us to would be greatly
appreciated.
                                        Romana Machado
                                        Geoff Dale
