minsky@media.mit.edu (Marvin Minsky) recently posted,

    edgar@spectrx.sbay.org (Edgar W. Swank) Recently Posted:

    >    Jason Jason <prophet@ksu.ksu.edu>:
    >    I am NOT a beleiver in Astrology, but I do feel the need to point
    >    out that the stars may very well produce some sort of effect that
    >    is not yet accounted for in the Einstein's gravity equations.

    Yes, and so might pronouncing the word "abracadabra".

Actually, "abracadabra" is likely to be more effective, at least at
producing some psychological expectation in anyone who hears it. Note
this was "prophet" Jason's quote, not mine.

    >The gravitational effects of the planets may also be too
    >small to effect human existence, but they are certainly many orders of
    >magnitude larger then those of the stars.

    And many orders of magnitude smaller than most other terrestrial
    events.

Except that whatever influence gravity from the other planets has
(including effects on the sun), it affects the entire Earth's surface
reasonably uniformly, while other terrestrial events have effects much
more local.

    >I read one speculation that a possible mechanism of influence of
    >planet positions would be tidal forces on the sun, which could alter
    >the sunspot cycle and the amount of radiation hitting the earth. This
    >could certainly affect human lives by chaotic effects on weather &
    >climate, floods, droughts, etc.

    The butterfly effect.  You're missing the entier pathetic stupidity of
    astrological belief.  ...

I don't think so. But astrology, as a crude attempt to correlate
astronomical observations with terrestrial ones, may have less claim
to "pathetic stupidity" than other belief systems which have even less
basis in observable facts. e.g. Christianity, Judaism, UFO's,
"technical analysis" of the stock market, ...

    Of course things can affect one another.  And of course, with
    chotic processes, small causes can have large effects *at much
    later times*.  But astrology assumes that certain small causes
    have enormous, overwhelming effects at the present instant of the
    moment of birth -- effects that are not drowned out by other
    causes, many orders of magnitude larger, and operating
    continuously at previous and later times.

I disagree that astrology claims what you say.  First, astrological
influences on an individual (if any) start at or shortly before
conception.  Astrology would perhaps be a more exact science if
date/time/place of conception rather than birth were used as the
primary datum for an individual. But the former is rarely recorded (:}
and is loosely correlated with the latter.

Second, astrological influences at conception or birth have the
lifetime of the individual to work themselves out.  Long enough, it
seems to me, for small causes to have observable effects.

One possible cause, hard to control for, is the "self-fulfilling
prophecy".  If an individual has some (possibly completely irrational)
belief in astrology (or something else) and an astrologer (or other
authority) tells him that he is supposed to have certain traits and
even that he is destined for good or bad fortune, he may unconsciously
find a way to make those predictions come true.

    >Finally, I've heard that astrology as as much art as science, and that
    >interpretation of the "signs" depends on empathy with the client (of
    >the professional astrologer).
    >
    >So the value of astrology may lie less with reading the "stars" than
    >just providing personal counseling and giving its followers an excuse
    >for failure ("the stars were against me"), much like psychology ("my
    >mother screwed me up") or sociology ("society screwed me up"), not to
    >mention religion ("my bad luck was all part of God's plan").

    Yes, astrological counselling can have effects -- but what leads
    you to assume it doesn't cause more failure than it excuses?

I don't, although I would expect market forces to weed out
astrologers who consistantly gave bad advice.

I recall hearing somewhere that the "cure" rate (for phychological
disorders) of witch doctors was about the same as for Western-trained
psychiatrists.

I don't think you have refuted my point that astrology (and other
belief systems) may have some value to their adherents quite apart
from their (lack of) scientific validity.

It seems to me that, unlike some belief systems, astrology is
falsifiable. As far as I am aware, no actual data has been gathered,
other than by the primordial astrologers; and I suspect their sample
size was much too small, as is the current survey on this list.

I would not be surprised if a survey of several million people (but
perhaps as small as 100,000) showed some statistically significant
correlations between date/time/place of birth and various observable
individual traits.  I would be very surprised if these correlations
matched those predicted by contemporary astrologers, although there is
always the phenomenon of the self-fulfilling prophecy to contend with.

Marvin, I'm flattered you chose my comment to respond to. [end of
grovel].

I think I rejoined the list after this thread started, apparently by a
post from you.  If you would kindly send me a copy of that post by
private e-mail, I might be able to comment upon it more intelligently.

Edgar Swank

