sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan) recently posted:

    Edgar W. Swank describes a speculative justification for astrology:

    >I read one speculation that a possible mechanism of influence of
    >planet positions would be tidal forces on the sun, which could alter
    >the sunspot cycle and the amount of radiation hitting the earth. This
    >could certainly affect human lives by chaotic effects on weather &
    >climate, floods, droughts, etc.

    It is possible to construct elaborate and sometimes humorous
    explanations for the ``validity'' of astrology.  But why stop
    there.  Many other long standing ``disciplines'' are waiting for
    attempts at rationalization.  For example:

    1) Divination of the future by examining the entrails of a
    sacrificed chicken.

    2) Prediction of the future by looking at tea leave patterns.

These are most easily "rationalized" as just vehicles to allow the
"natural" pre-cognitive abilities of the practitioner to operate. Any
random pattern would do, e.g. Rorschach test.

A more direct method of predicting the future is via dreams.  There is
a book, probably in the library, written around 1930, I believe,
called "An Experiment with [in?] Time by [J.  W. ?] Dunne.  Anyone can
perform the experiment for themselves as described in the book.  Dunne
describes the many possibilities for false positive results and
nonetheless asserts the he experienced genuine pre-cognition.  I tried
it in High School, & got one dream that might be classified as
genuinely pre-cognitive.  Your mileage may vary.

    3) Insight into the character and mental capacity of an individual
    by studying the bumps on a persons head, i.e., phrenology

This is at least plausible.  I would put it in the same class as
astrology.  Falsifiable, but the data to do so has not been collected.

    These methods are currently not as popular as astrology.  But they
    have been very popular in the past and the phenomenon of
    popularity can exhibit an intriguing cyclicity.  After
    constructing your theory you should also consider the following
    alternative/complementary theory:  The main reason these
    disciplines were invented was to allow the manipulation of the
    gullible by charlatans for monetary gain.  gfs

Perhaps so, but the presence of some charlatans does not mean that
every investigator is one.  Certainly the CSICOP practice of having
all experiments examined by a professional magician is a good one. EWS

